{%- comment -%}
  ═══ LBASS — PRICE BLOCK (2026-09-09) ═══

  SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH for rendering a price anywhere a product is listed.
  Collection grid, search, homepage rails, look cards, vault — all of them.

  ── WHY THIS EXISTS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Price markup was written independently in ~7 places and had already drifted.
  Measured 2026-09-09: templates/index.liquid and templates/page.vault.liquid
  rendered compare-at + savings, while templates/collection.liquid:341 rendered
  `{{ product.price | money }}` and nothing else. A genuinely marked-down piece
  showed its discount on the homepage and hid it on the collection grid — the
  page a shopper actually browses. Same catalogue, two answers.

  ── THE ONLY LEGITIMATE STRIKETHROUGH ────────────────────────────────────────
  A struck-through price renders if and ONLY if Shopify's own
  `variant.compare_at_price` is set AND is greater than the price being charged.
  That is a former price the shop actually asked for. Nothing else qualifies.

  The saving and the percentage are DERIVED from those two numbers, never passed
  in and never stored. That is what makes «‑33%» and «199 DH  299 DH» incapable
  of contradicting each other: one subtraction, one division, same source.

  NEVER render a strikethrough from:
    • the running campaign      — it discounts the 2nd item, not this one
                                  (see snippets/lbass-promo.liquid)
    • lbass.retail_ref          — what the model costs NEW. Plain text only.
                                  templates/product.liquid:700 states the rule:
                                  a figure that was never our price is
                                  misrepresentation and risks Merchant Listings.

  ── ROUNDING ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Liquid's `divided_by` floors on integers, so a naive percentage reads low
  (31.7% would print as 31). Percentages here go through per-mille and round
  half-up, so the badge matches what a shopper gets dividing the two printed
  numbers themselves.

  ── INHERITING THE CARD TYPOGRAPHY, NOT FIGHTING IT ──────────────────────────
  On a collection card the container keeps its `card-price` class on purpose.
  snippets/lbass-stamp.liquid:267 styles `.grid .card .card-price` with the
  display face at 22px in `--lb-ink` on the bone card, all `!important`. That is
  the live design, so the current price INHERITS it — this file sets no colour
  and no font on `.lb-price__now`. Only the new children (struck price, percent
  pill, savings line) carry their own type, and a rule on a child beats an
  inherited value from the parent without needing `!important`.

  Both existing sheets also force `direction:ltr` on `.card-price`, so the row
  lays out now → was → percent left-to-right. That is correct and matches the
  reference design; digits read LTR even on the RTL storefront. The savings line
  sits OUTSIDE the container so it follows the card's RTL flow.

  ── CURRENCY ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  `divided_by: 100.0` converts centimes to the schema.org decimal. It assumes a
  two-decimal currency, which MAD is. Centralised HERE so a future zero-decimal
  market is one edit, not seven. ISO code comes from `cart.currency.iso_code` —
  never the hardcoded "MAD" the old collection schema emitted.

  ── PARAMETERS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    product    required
    variant    optional — defaults to selected_or_first_available_variant
    microdata  optional — true (default) emits schema.org/Offer
    context    optional — 'card' (default) | 'plain'

  USAGE
      {% render 'lbass-price', product: product %}
      {% render 'lbass-price', product: product, variant: v, microdata: false %}
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign lb_v = variant | default: product.selected_or_first_available_variant
  assign lb_price = lb_v.price | default: product.price
  assign lb_cap = lb_v.compare_at_price

  assign lb_on_sale = false
  if lb_cap and lb_cap > lb_price
    assign lb_on_sale = true
    comment
      Exact, no rounding to get wrong, so it stays inline.
    endcomment
    assign lb_save = lb_cap | minus: lb_price
    comment
      The PERCENTAGE is not computed here. snippets/lbass-sale owns it, because
      the badge on the card image prints the same number and the two must not be
      able to disagree. `pct` may be passed in by a caller that already asked
      (the collection card does, once, instead of 250 nested renders).
    endcomment
    assign lb_pct = pct
    if lb_pct == blank
      capture lb_pct
        render 'lbass-sale', product: product, variant: lb_v
      endcapture
    endif
    assign lb_pct = lb_pct | strip
  endif

  assign lb_micro = microdata
  if microdata == blank
    assign lb_micro = true
  endif

  assign lb_ctx = context | default: 'card'
  assign lb_cls = 'lb-price'
  if lb_ctx == 'card'
    assign lb_cls = 'card-price lb-price'
  endif
  if lb_on_sale
    assign lb_cls = lb_cls | append: ' is-sale'
  endif
-%}

<div class="{{ lb_cls }}"
  {%- if lb_micro %} itemprop="offers" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Offer"{% endif -%}>
  {%- if lb_micro -%}
    <meta itemprop="priceCurrency" content="{{ cart.currency.iso_code }}">
    <meta itemprop="price" content="{{ lb_price | divided_by: 100.0 }}">
    {%- comment -%}
      <meta>, not an empty <span>. The container is a flex row with a gap, and
      an empty span is still a FLEX ITEM: it contributed a full column gap of
      invisible width between the microdata and the price. Measured at 360px
      that phantom 6px was exactly what pushed the reference price onto a second
      line — the price pair fits in 128px of a 130px row, but not in 134px.
      <meta> carries identical microdata (Google reads the content attribute,
      not the box) and is display:none in the UA stylesheet, so it is not a flex
      item at all. The two <meta> tags above were already written this way.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <meta itemprop="availability" content="https://schema.org/{% if product.available %}InStock{% else %}OutOfStock{% endif %}">
  {%- endif -%}

  <span class="lb-price__now">{{ lb_price | money }}</span>

  {%- if lb_on_sale -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      `<s>` is not announced by most screen readers, so without these labels a
      non-sighted shopper hears "494 dirhams 549 dirhams minus 10 percent" and
      has no way to tell which number they pay. The labels are the only thing
      that disambiguates it; they cost nothing visually.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <s class="lb-price__was"><span class="lb-sr">بدل </span>{{ lb_cap | money }}</s>
    {%- comment -%}
      A markdown too small to round to 1% is still a real markdown — 1000 -> 999
      genuinely happened and «وفّر 1 درهم» is true — so the struck price and the
      saving stay. The PERCENTAGE does not: «‑0%» is not a discount claim, it is
      a rounding artefact, and it reads as a mistake. Caught by
      test/render/test_sale_presentation.rb, which asserts no surface can ever
      print it.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if lb_pct != '0' -%}
      <span class="lb-price__off"><span class="lb-sr">تخفيض </span>&minus;{{ lb_pct }}%</span>
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endif -%}
</div>

{%- if lb_on_sale -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    Outside the price container so it inherits the card's RTL direction rather
    than the container's forced ltr. The amount itself stays ltr.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <p class="lb-price__save" dir="auto">{{ settings.promo_save_label | default: 'وفّر' }} <span dir="ltr">{{ lb_save | money }}</span></p>
{%- endif -%}
